    "start_byte": 16461,
    "text": "theorem not_finiteDimensional : ¬ FiniteDimensional ℂ Limit"
  },
  "content": {
    "assumptions": "The algebra $A$ is the fixed completed CAR algebra. For every $n\\geq0$, its canonical stage map $\\iota_n:A_n\\to A$ is complex-linear and injective, and $\\dim_{\\mathbb C}A_n=(2^n)^2$. These are established properties of this construction, not new assumptions on an arbitrary algebra.",
    "conclusion": "No finite integer can be the complex dimension of $A$: every stage contributes an embedded vector space of dimension $4^n$, and these dimensions are unbounded.",
    "content_completeness": "CARD_CONTENT_COMPLETE",
    "definition_explanation": null,
    "display_title": "The completed CAR algebra is infinite-dimensional",
    "language_tag": "en",
    "lean_realization_notes": "The conclusion concerns vector-space dimension over $\\mathbb C$. It does not require an irreducible representation or a claim about compact operators.",
    "natural_language_statement": "Let $A$ be the norm completion of the matrix system $A_n=M_{2^n}(\\mathbb C)$ with embeddings $a\\mapsto a\\otimes I_2$. Then $A$ is not finite-dimensional as a complex vector space.",
    "one_sentence_role": "Rules out finite dimension by retaining matrix subalgebras of unbounded dimension.",
    "proof_steps": [
      {
        "step_id": "s1",
        "text": "Assume that $A$ is finite-dimensional and write $k=\\dim_{\\mathbb C}A$."
      },
      {
        "step_id": "s2",
        "text": "The linear injection $\\iota_{k+1}$ forces $\\dim_{\\mathbb C}A_{k+1}\\leq k$."
      },
      {
        "step_id": "s3",
        "text": "The stage dimension is $(2^{k+1})^2>k$, yielding the contradiction."
      }
    ],
    "proof_summary": "If $A$ had finite dimension $k$, injectivity of each $\\iota_n$ would give $(2^n)^2\\leq k$. Take $n=k+1$. The elementary inequality $k<2^n\\leq(2^n)^2$ contradicts that bound."
  },
